# Integrality criterion making X θ j a weight-two cusp form

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.exists_cuspForm_qExpansion_eq_mul_thetaL_of_isIntegral`
(`51fe59e7-e662-5bf4-ab8e-93e14458f18f`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `f224858c-cd3c-5edb-b878-ea5eca81ae56`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number and let $J :=$ `coeffEmb ℂ jq` denote the image in $\mathbb
C((q))$, under the coefficientwise ring map induced by $\mathbb Q \to \mathbb C$, of the Laurent
series `jq` $= q^{-1}\cdot$ (the rational power series `jNumQ`), i.e. the $q$-expansion of the
modular invariant $j$. Let $X \in \mathbb C((q))$ be an element of `laurentBaseChange ℂ
(modularFunctionFieldFull N)`, the intermediate field of $\mathbb C((q))$ obtained by adjoining to
$\mathbb C$ the coefficientwise images of the subfield of $\mathbb Q((q))$ generated over $\mathbb
Q$ by the series $q$-rescalings `qExpand ℚ d jq` for the nonzero divisors $d$ of $N$. Let $M$ be a
natural number, and assume two integrality hypotheses: first, $X^{6} J^{4}(J - 1728)^{3}$ is
integral over the $\mathbb C$-subalgebra of $\mathbb C((q))$ generated by $J$; second, $X^{2M}
J^{M+1}(J-1728)^{M}$ is integral over the $\mathbb C$-subalgebra generated by $J^{-1}$. Then there
exists a cusp form $f$ of weight $2$ for $\Gamma_0(N)$ whose $q$-expansion of period $1$, viewed as
an element of $\mathbb C((q))$, equals $X \cdot \theta J$, where $\theta$ is the $\mathbb C$-linear
operator `thetaL` sending a Laurent series to $q$ times its formal derivative.

This is the analytic core of the implication that a regular differential on $X_0(N)$ gives a
weight-two cusp form, stated purely in terms of $q$-expansions: the two integrality conditions
encode regularity at the finite points and at the cusps, with no place, valuation or differential
appearing. It is used in the passage from elements of the space of regular differentials, presented
by their $q$-expansions, to cusp forms in $S_2(\Gamma_0(N))$.

